# Example (English): how AI Transparent labels an AI-generated image.
# AITL keywords work in English too; the report is then written in English.

explain "How an AI Transparent label is made"
  organisation: AI Transparent
  system: AI Transparent labelling service 1.0
  version: 2026-09-28
  audience: anyone who receives a labelled image
  language: en
  summary: When an organisation labels an AI image, AI Transparent adds several marks to the file.
    You can see some of them, others are hidden in the file. Anyone can check the label for free.

purpose
  plain: Make it clear, for everyone, that an image was made or edited with AI, as the EU AI Act requires.

term fingerprint
  plain: A short code that is calculated from the file. Change one pixel and the code changes completely.
  technical: SHA-256 hash over the file bytes.
  metaphor: Like a real fingerprint: unique for each file.
    limit: Real fingerprints can be found on objects. A file fingerprint only tells you whether you have exactly the same file.

term content credentials
  also: C2PA
  plain: A standard way to store where a file comes from, sealed inside the file.

step "Visible label"
  plain: A small badge is placed in the corner of the image, saying it was made with AI.
  technical: Badge rendered with GD onto the image pixels, bottom-left by default.

step "Hidden label in the file"
  plain: Information about the AI origin is written inside the file, where software can read it.
  technical: XMP metadata with IPTC DigitalSourceType trainedAlgorithmicMedia.

step "Content Credentials with a seal"
  plain: The label is also stored as content credentials and sealed with a digital signature and a time stamp.
  technical: C2PA 2.x manifest, signed with ES256 and an RFC 3161 time stamp from DigiCert.
  metaphor: Like a sealed envelope with a postmark: you can see if someone opened it, and when it was sent.
    limit: An envelope can be thrown away. If someone removes the credentials from the file, the other marks and the register still exist.

step "Entry in the register"
  plain: The fingerprint of the file is stored in a public register, so the label can still be found if the file loses its hidden information.
  technical: Registry entry with the SHA-256 fingerprint and an Ed25519-signed manifest.

outcome "A labelled image"
  plain: A copy of the image that carries a visible badge, hidden labels and a verifiable seal.

limitation "Screenshots"
  plain: If someone takes a screenshot, the hidden marks and the seal are lost. The visible badge stays, unless it is cut away.

limitation "Not a detector"
  plain: The service shows that a label was added. It cannot tell whether an unlabelled image was made with AI.

oversight
  plain: The organisation that labels the image is responsible for the label. Anyone can check a label at app.aitransparent.eu.
  contact: info@learningtour.nl
